%I A398676 #12 Aug 12 2026 10:44:33
%S A398676 1,2,6,26,46,58,74,86,106,146,151,158,166,186,202,206,214,226,242,266,
%T A398676 298,311,326,346,354,361,362,366,382,386,394,421,426,431,446,458,466,
%U A398676 506,526,541,554,566,571,578,586,606,626,634,641,662,666,674,706,718,746,751,762,766,786
%N A398676 Numbers k such that sigma(3*k+2) > sigma(3*k), where sigma(k) is the sum of divisors of k (A000203).
%C A398676 Numbers k such that A144615(k) > A144613(k).
%C A398676 The asymptotic density of this sequence exists and is in the interval [0.0361784, 0.1784994] (Luo and Ren, 2023).
%C A398676 The numbers of terms that do not exceed 10^k, for k = 1, 2, ..., are 3, 8, 71, 721, 7053, 70095, 699548, 6987700, 69840621, ... . It appears that the asymptotic density of this sequence equals 0.0698... .
%H A398676 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A398676/b398676.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398676 Xin-qi Luo and Chen-kai Ren, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.00295">On the natural density of integers n for which sigma(kn + r_1) > sigma(kn + r_2)</a>, arXiv:2311.00295 [math.NT], 2023-2026.
%e A398676 1 is a term since sigma(3*1+2) = 6 > sigma(3*1) = 4.
%t A398676 q[k_] := DivisorSigma[1, 3*k + 2] > DivisorSigma[1, 3*k]; Select[Range[800], q]
%o A398676 (PARI) isok(k) = sigma(3*k+2) > sigma(3*k);
%Y A398676 Cf. A000203, A082957, A144613, A144615, A325423, A398677.
%K A398676 nonn,easy,new
